40 requests, run concurrently the way traffic arrives.

  min     2590ms  ########                      
  p50     3078ms  ##########                    
  mean    3905ms  ############                    <- most dashboards
  p90     5798ms  ##################            
  p95     8033ms  #########################     
  p99     9657ms  ##############################
  max     9657ms  ##############################

  requests slower than the mean: 13 of 40 (32%)
  p99 is 3.1x the median
  (with 40 requests the p99 is simply the slowest one; a p99 needs hundreds of requests to mean anything)

The mean sits where nobody lives. This distribution has a long right tail —
agents that took an extra step, questions that needed two tools instead of
one — and a tail pulls a mean upward without describing anything.

Report the mean and you are quoting a figure that 68% of requests beat,
so most people think you are being pessimistic; meanwhile the 32% having a
genuinely bad time are invisible inside it.

Percentiles say something you can act on. p50 is the typical request. p95 is
the one a user notices and mentions. p99 is the one that produces a ticket.

Two rules that follow, and the second one gets broken constantly:

  never average a percentile      the mean of two p95s is not a p95, and a
                                  dashboard that rolls hourly p95s into a
                                  daily one is showing you a number with no
                                  meaning at all
  bucket before you aggregate     a p95 across every request is dominated by
                                  whichever kind is slowest and most common

  Which matters here:

    unanswerable   10 requests   p50   4939ms   p95   9657ms
    composite       6 requests   p50   4257ms   p95   8332ms
    document       14 requests   p50   3016ms   p95   3457ms
    warehouse      10 requests   p50   2915ms   p95   3201ms

The slowest population's p95 is 3.0x the fastest one's, and a single
overall p95 of 8033ms sits between them describing neither. If the slow kind is
the one your best customers use, that number is not merely uninformative — it
is reassuring, which is worse.
